.Dd September 28, 2026
.Dt ROBFILE 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm robfile
.Nd what robbie makes, and how
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A robfile tells
.Xr robbie 1
what can be made in a directory: which file or task each rule makes,
what it needs, and the shell script, its recipe, that makes it.
It also holds variables, and can include other robfiles.
.Pp
The file is read a line at a time.
A line ending in a backslash goes on to the next, as in
.Xr sh 1 ;
it is one logical line, known by the number of its first line.
Spaces and tabs at the end of a line do not matter; at its start they
indent it.
Each logical line is one of:
.Bl -tag -width "name: inputs" -offset indent
.It blank
Ignored.
.It Li # Ar comment
A comment, at the left margin.
.It Ar name Li = Ar value
A variable.
.It Ar target Ns Li : Ar inputs
The first line of a rule.
.It Li include Ar file
Another robfile, read in place.
.It indented
A line of the recipe of the rule above.
.El
.Pp
A line at the margin that is not a comment or an include is a rule or
a variable by whichever of
.Ql \&:
and
.Ql =
comes first, so
.Ql path = /bin:/usr/bin
is a variable and
.Ql prog: a=b
a rule.
Outside a recipe, a backslash, the line break after it and the blanks
around them become one space.
.Ss Comments and descriptions
A comment is a whole line, and never goes on to the next, even when it
ends in a backslash.
A comment on the line just above a rule is that rule's description,
which
.Xr robbie 1
shows in its listing.
Other comments are ignored.
.Ss Variables
.Bd -literal -offset indent
name = value
.Ed
.Pp
sets the variable
.Ar name .
A name is a letter or underscore followed by letters, digits and
underscores.
The value is the rest of the line, less the blanks at either end, and
may be empty.
.Pp
Placeholders in the value are filled in as the line is read, from the
variables as they stand at that line, so
.Ql flags = {flags} -g
adds to the value of
.Va flags
set above it.
A variable set again keeps its place and takes the new value.
.Pp
A variable set on the command line, as in
.Ql robbie prefix=/usr ,
is fixed: the robfile's lines setting it are ignored.
.Ss Rules
.Bd -literal -offset indent
target: inputs
    recipe
.Ed
.Pp
A rule makes one
.Ar target
from its
.Ar inputs ,
a list of words, which may be empty.
The target is a file, and is out of date, and remade, when it is
missing, when it is older than one of its inputs, or when one of its
inputs is out of date itself.
Times are compared to the nanosecond, and a file exactly as old as an
input is up to date.
An input that no rule makes must exist.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
task target: inputs
    recipe
.Ed
.Pp
makes a task instead: a target that is not a file, whose recipe runs
whenever it is asked for.
A task is always out of date, so a file that needs one is remade every
time too.
.Ql task
is a word of its own only when a target follows it, so
.Ql task:
is a rule for a file called
.Pa task .
.Pp
Placeholders in the target, the inputs and the description are filled
in as the line is read, as in a variable's value.
The target must then be one word, and no two rules may make the same
target, whether file or task, except patterns, described below.
.Pp
No target may need itself, whether directly, as in
.Ql prog: prog ,
or through the inputs of other rules.
Such a loop can never be made, and is an error wherever it is in the
robfile, even when nothing asked for goes through it.
.Ss Globs
An input of a plain rule with a
.Ql *
in it is a glob, and stands for the files it matches, sorted by name,
as in
.Xr sh 1 :
.Ql *.c
matches every file ending in
.Pa .c ,
except those starting with a dot, and
.Ql src/*.c
those in
.Pa src .
Only
.Ql *
is special;
.Ql \&? ,
.Ql \&[
and
.Ql \e
are letters like any other.
A glob is matched each time
.Xr robbie 1
runs, after placeholders are filled in, so a variable can hold one.
.Pp
A glob that matches nothing is an error, wherever it is in the
robfile, since it is nearly always a typo.
A glob can only name files that exist, not files still to be made.
.Pp
A rule needs each input once, in the order first named, however many
times it is named or matched, so in
.Ql prog: main.c *.c
the file
.Pa main.c
comes first, and once.
.Ss Patterns
A rule whose target has a
.Ql *
in it is a pattern:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
*.o: *.c config.h
    cc -c {input} -o {target}
.Ed
.Pp
makes any file whose name fits
.Ql *.o ,
such as
.Pa main.o ,
and the
.Ql *
in each of its inputs stands for the same text, the stem, here
.Ql main ,
so
.Pa main.o
needs
.Pa main.c
and
.Pa config.h .
The stem is at least one byte, and may hold a
.Ql / ,
and the directory can change with it, as in
.Ql build/*.o: src/*.c .
A pattern has one
.Ql * .
In its inputs a
.Ql *
is the stem, never a glob.
.Pp
A name that no plain rule makes is matched against the patterns, in
the order of the robfile, and the first that fits it, and whose inputs
all exist or can be made in turn, makes it.
So several patterns can make the same kind of file, as in
.Bd -literal -offset indent
*.o: *.c
    cc -c {input}
*.o: *.s
    as {input} -o {target}
.Ed
.Pp
where an object is made from its C file if there is one, and
otherwise from its assembler.
Patterns can make each other's inputs, as when a C file is itself made
from a grammar, but a pattern is not tried again for anything it needs,
however indirectly, so the search always ends.
A plain rule always wins over a pattern.
A name that only a pattern makes can be named on the command line.
.Ss Recipes
The recipe is the indented lines under a rule: a shell script, which
.Xr robbie 1
hands to
.Xr sh 1
whole, not a line at a time, as
.Ql /bin/sh -e -c Ar script .
So a loop can span lines, a
.Ql cd
carries on to the lines after it, and the first command that fails
stops the recipe, and the build.
Tabs and spaces both indent.
.Pp
Each line of a recipe loses the indentation it shares with the
recipe's first line, and keeps the rest, byte for byte, so the body of
a loop can be indented further; a tab is not shared with spaces.
Blank lines between recipe lines are kept, for here-documents; those
before the first line and after the last are not.
A line ending in a backslash keeps it, with its line break, for the
shell to join.
.Pp
An indented comment is a line of the recipe, for the shell.
A comment at the margin is the robfile's, and is dropped; the recipe
goes on under it.
A variable, a rule or an include at the margin ends the recipe.
.Pp
Placeholders in a recipe are filled in only when it runs, after the
whole robfile has been read, so a recipe sees the final value of each
variable, wherever the recipe stands.
Each recipe is checked for unknown names before anything runs.
A recipe can also use robbie's own placeholders, which name parts of
the rule being made:
.Bl -tag -width "{inputs}" -offset indent
.It Li {target}
the file or task being made
.It Li {input}
its first input, or nothing when it has none
.It Li {inputs}
all its inputs, each once, in the order first named, separated by
spaces
.It Li {newer}
those of its inputs that are newer than the target, as they stand when
the recipe runs; for a target that does not exist yet, or a task, that
is all of them, and an input that is a task always counts as newer
.It Li {stem}
what the
.Ql *
stood for, in the recipe of a pattern, and an error anywhere else
.El
.Ss Placeholders
A name in braces,
.Ql {name} ,
is a placeholder, and is replaced by the value of the variable
.Ar name .
Apart from comprehensions, described below, nothing else is one, so
shell code passes through untouched:
.Ql {}
and
.Ql { print $1 }
are not placeholders, and a brace straight after a
.Ql $ ,
as in
.Ql ${HOME} ,
belongs to the shell.
robbie never touches
.Ql $ .
Two opening braces,
.Ql {{ ,
stand for one, for the rare shell code that looks like a placeholder.
.Pp
Placeholders are filled in in one pass, from left to right: a value
that has been filled in is not looked at again, so braces in it are
kept.
A value set on the command line is used as it is, and its braces are
kept too.
.Pp
An unknown name is an error, since it is nearly always a typo, and the
message suggests the closest known name when one is close enough.
The names
.Va target ,
.Va input ,
.Va inputs ,
.Va newer
and
.Va stem
are robbie's own: they name parts of the rule being made, and can be
used only in a recipe.
They cannot be variable names.
.Ss Comprehensions
A comprehension is a placeholder that stands for a list:
.Bl -tag -width "{*.o for *.c in name}" -offset indent
.It Li {*.o for *.c}
a
.Pa .o
for each
.Pa .c
file on disk
.It Li {*.o for *.c in Ar name Ns Li }
a
.Pa .o
for each word of the placeholder
.Ar name
that ends in
.Pa .c
.El
.Pp
The words are in the order of Python's list comprehensions.
The
.Ql *
means what it means in a pattern, the stem, so the directory can
change too:
.Ql {build/*.o for src/*.c} .
Both words must hold a
.Ql * ,
the second only one, and they are separated from
.Ql for
and
.Ql in
by blanks, with none just inside the braces; anything else is not a
comprehension, and passes through untouched.
.Pp
The first form finds the files as a glob does, sorted by name, and it
is an error when there are none.
In the second, words that don't fit are left out, and the list may
come out empty;
.Ar name
can be a variable, or, in a recipe, one of robbie's own placeholders,
as in
.Ql {*.d for *.c in inputs} .
A comprehension can name files that don't exist yet, which a glob
can't, so a program can need an object for each of its sources, and
a pattern make them.
.Ss Including other robfiles
.Bd -literal -offset indent
include file
.Ed
.Pp
reads the robfile
.Ar file
as if its lines stood in place of the include: its variables and rules
join the others, in order, and an included file can include others.
Placeholders in
.Ar file
are filled in first.
It is found from the directory
.Xr robbie 1
runs in, as every other path in a robfile is, not from the directory
of the file that includes it.
A file that includes itself, however indirectly, is an error.
.Pp
.Ql include
is a word of its own only when a file name follows it, so
.Ql include: x
is a rule and
.Ql include = x
a variable.
.Ss Coming from make
A plain Makefile turns into a robfile by hand, a line at a time:
.Bl -column "$(patsubst %.c,%.o,$(SRC))" "{*.o for *.c in SRC}" -offset indent
.It Sy make Ta Sy robfile
.It Li $@ Ta Li {target}
.It Li $< Ta Li {input}
.It Li $^ Ta Li {inputs}
.It Li $? Ta Li {newer}
.It Li $* Ta Li {stem}
.It Li $(X) , Li ${X} Ta Li {X}
.It Li $$ Ta Li $
.It Li X := y Ta Li X = y
.It Li X += y Ta Li X = {X} y
.It Li .PHONY: t Ta Li task t:
.It Li %.o: %.c Ta Li *.o: *.c
.It Li $(wildcard *.c) Ta Li *.c
.It Li $(SRC:.c=.o) Ta Li {*.o for *.c in SRC}
.It Li $(patsubst %.c,%.o,$(SRC)) Ta Li {*.o for *.c in SRC}
.It Li include f Ta Li include f
.El
.Pp
A recipe is one script, so its lines no longer need
.Ql ;\e
to share a shell, and they may be indented with spaces.
A variable is filled in as its line is read, so it cannot use one set
further down, as make's
.Ql =
can.
There is nothing for make's functions, conditionals, recursive calls
or implicit rules: write them out, or leave them to the shell.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The robfile that builds robbie itself:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# Robfile for robbie: build it, test it, install it.

raz    = raz -Werror
prefix = /usr/local
pages  = doc/robbie.1 doc/robfile.5

# The program itself.
build/robbie: src/*.raz
    mkdir -p build
    {raz} -o {target} src/Robbie.raz

# A release tarball.
robbie.tar.gz: build/robbie {pages} README.md
    tar czf {target} {inputs}

# Run the tests, then check the man pages.
task test: build/robbie
    for t in test/*.sh; do
        sh "$t" | diff -u "${t%.sh}.out" - || exit 1
    done
    # groff exits 0 even when it warns, so any output is a failure
    out=$(groff -mdoc -ww -z {pages} 2>&1)
    test -z "$out" || { echo "$out"; exit 1; }

# Install into {prefix}.
task install: build/robbie
    install -Dm755 build/robbie "$DESTDIR{prefix}/bin/robbie"
    install -Dm644 doc/robbie.1 "$DESTDIR{prefix}/share/man/man1/robbie.1"
    install -Dm644 doc/robfile.5 "$DESTDIR{prefix}/share/man/man5/robfile.5"

# Remove what install put there.
task uninstall:
    rm -f "$DESTDIR{prefix}/bin/robbie" \e
          "$DESTDIR{prefix}/share/man/man1/robbie.1" \e
          "$DESTDIR{prefix}/share/man/man5/robfile.5"

# Remove everything the build made.
task clean:
    rm -rf build robbie.tar.gz
.Ed
.Pp
The robfile for dwm, a C program built from an object for each C
file:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# Robfile for dwm: a C program, one object per source file.

cc = cc -Os

# The window manager.
dwm: {*.o for *.c}
    {cc} -o {target} {inputs} -lX11 -lXft

# Each object from its C file.
*.o: *.c config.h
    {cc} -c {input} -o {target}
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr robbie 1 ,
.Xr make 1 ,
.Xr sh 1
